# BioKey Citizen Science Study Protocol v1.0

## Background
Recent theories suggest aging may result from loss of bioelectric information 
(Levin, 2024). We hypothesize that synchronized group meditation during sleep 
may enhance bioelectric coherence and improve health markers.

## Objectives
PRIMARY: Assess feasibility of group-synchronized sleep meditation
SECONDARY: Explore effects on sleep quality, HRV, and well-being

## Design
- Type: Prospective observational cohort
- Registration: Open, rolling enrollment
- Groups: 
  * Group A: Synchronized meditation (coordinated timing)
  * Group B: Individual meditation (own timing)
  * Group C: Passive monitoring (no meditation)

## Participants
- Inclusion: Age 18+, smartphone, wearable device
- Exclusion: None (all welcome)
- Informed Consent: Digital consent via app

## Intervention
GROUP A (Synchronized):
- Meditation audio during deep sleep (N3 phase)
- Timing: Coordinated across group (±15 min window)
- Frequency: 3x/week (new moon, full moon, + midpoint)
- Content: "I am for health and longevity" (15 sec, loop 3x)

GROUP B (Individual):
- Same audio and frequency
- Timing: Not synchronized with group

GROUP C (Control):
- Sleep monitoring only
- No audio intervention

## Duration
- 12 weeks (3 lunar cycles)
- Weekly data uploads
- Monthly questionnaires

## Measurements
AUTOMATIC (from wearables):
- Total sleep time
- Sleep stages (light, deep, REM)
- Heart rate & HRV (RMSSD, SDNN)
- Steps, activity level

SELF-REPORT (app questionnaires):
DAILY:
- Sleep quality (1-10 scale)
- Morning energy (1-10 scale)
- Evening stress (1-10 scale)

WEEKLY:
- WHO-5 Well-Being Index
- Sleep quality (PSQI short form)

MONTHLY:
- Perceived Stress Scale (PSS-4)
- Health status (SF-12)

## Data Collection
- Wearable data: Automatic sync via app
- Questionnaires: In-app forms
- Storage: Encrypted, GDPR-compliant
- De-identification: Automatic (participant ID only)

## Analysis Plan
PRIMARY OUTCOMES:
- Feasibility: % completing 12 weeks
- Adherence: % nights with successful intervention

EXPLORATORY OUTCOMES:
- Change in sleep quality (PSQI)
- Change in HRV (RMSSD)
- Change in well-being (WHO-5)
- Group differences (A vs B vs C)

STATISTICAL METHODS:
- Descriptive statistics
- Mixed-effects models (account for repeated measures)
- Intention-to-treat analysis
- Subgroup analyses (group size effects)

## Ethics
- Minimal risk observational study
- Digital informed consent
- Right to withdraw anytime
- Data privacy (GDPR Article 89)
- No IRB required (citizen science exemption)

